  (:adr/authoritative_for
  (vector "Readiness of a cloud-itonami sub-repo is established by EXECUTING its test suite (clojure -M:dev:test, or -M:test where no :dev alias exists), never by looking for an ':implemented' keyword in its README. The keyword heuristic was measured wrong in both directions and must not be reintroduced as a readiness signal."
   "Any sweep that measures repo state must record WHICH tree it measured. Local west checkouts drift from upstream; a verdict from a stale tree is not a verdict about the repo. Where local and upstream disagree, upstream HEAD wins, because upstream is what a forker receives."
   "isco-* blueprints are scored from ILOSTAT occupation data at ISCO-08 MAJOR GROUP (1 digit) resolution only. There is no global 2- or 4-digit employment series, so repos sharing a first digit necessarily receive an identical figure and this must be labelled as a tie, not presented as per-occupation precision."
   "ISCO-08 major groups 0, 6 and 9 stay unscored. The ILO 'World' aggregate has no employment value for them in any year of the series, so no global figure exists to cite."))

  (:adr/decision
  "1. Replace the README-keyword readiness heuristic with executed test suites across all 913 product repos. 2. Treat checkout staleness as a first-class source of error: quantify it, re-run stale repos at upstream HEAD, and let upstream supersede local. 3. Score isco-* from ILOSTAT (World employment x median USD earnings x 12) at major-group resolution with the tie and upward-bias limits recorded on every row; leave groups 0/6/9 unscored. 4. Publish a price list for cloud-itonami-isic-7310, the top zero-capital-feasible blueprint, without creating any Stripe object -- that is an owner action. 5. Fix the three genuine defects the sweep surfaced, each as its own PR, and leave them unmerged for review.")

  (:adr/artifacts
  "{:prs [\"cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-7310#1 -- publish a price list (Managed Starter JPY 80,000/mo, matching the tier live on isic-6399), market-anchored, no Stripe object created\"
          \"cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-2660#1 -- :phase/N keywords are unreadable on the JVM, so the medical-device governor suite had never run; 0 tests read as 0 failures\"
          \"cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-3812#1 -- client-manifest filtered an atom, and on the wrong key depth\"
          \"cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-6492#1 -- an absent loan application read back as a record of nils instead of nil\"]
    :artifact \"https://claude.ai/code/artifact/d3404238-2313-4c6c-8923-7238788a4984 -- sortable ledger of all 1,226 repos with measured readiness, cited market size, and confidence tier per row\"
    :repaired \"orgs/kotoba-lang/identity had all 17 tracked files deleted from its working tree (no modifications, no untracked files), which build-blocked isic-6493 and iso3166-jpn. Restored with git checkout -- . ; both then passed (82 and 42 tests).\"}")

  (:adr/body
  "# Context

An earlier pass ranked all 1,226 cloud-itonami sub-repos by
`log10(addressable_market_usd) x readiness`. Two inputs were weak, and both were
called out at the time rather than hidden: `readiness` came from grepping each
README for an `:implemented` keyword, and the 251 `isco-*` rows had no market
number at all and were flagged `unscored`.

A 20-repo workflow audit then found that 16 of 20 repos it doubted were fully
built and green -- an ~80% false-negative rate. That made the heuristic
indefensible as anything but a placeholder, so this ADR records replacing it.

# What was measured

All 913 product repos (`isic-*` 439, `isco-*` 251, `iso3166-*` 223) had their
real suite executed. Reference catalogs (`lei-*`, `assoc-*`, `municipality-*`,
313 rows) were left out: they are read-only compliance-fact sources feeding the
products (ADR-2607141700) and are not independently monetizable.

Readiness is now taken from the observed result:

    pass     -> 1.00   suite runs green
    fail     -> 0.70   implemented, suite runs, some assertions fail
    error    -> 0.50   code present, suite fails to load
    no-tests -> 0.30   deps + src, no test suite
    no-deps  -> 0.05   scaffold shell

# The staleness correction, which changed verdicts

The first sweep ran against LOCAL west checkouts. A batched GraphQL query against
upstream default-branch OIDs found 92 of the 913 trees behind upstream. That is
not a cosmetic caveat: `isco-2111` was recorded `fail` locally while its
`origin/main` is green, because the local tree sat two commits before a commit
titled 'fix(advisor): propagate finalized?/novel? claim flags into the proposal'.

All 92 were re-run in a detached worktree at upstream HEAD. Seven verdicts
flipped -- `isco-2111` fail->pass, and six repos recorded `no-deps` locally
(`isic-4321`, `-5011`, `-5110`, `-8020`, `-8130`, `iso3166-hrv`) are fully built
upstream.

Two further classes of false negative came from the harness rather than the
repos, and were eliminated rather than reported as defects:

- six repos (`isic-6411/6420/6511/6512/6810/6920`) resolve a sibling
  `:local/root \"../cloud-itonami-isic-8291\"`, which was absent beside the
  scratchpad worktrees. With the sibling linked, all six pass.
- `isic-6493` and `iso3166-jpn` failed on a missing manifest for
  `kotoba-lang/identity`. That library's working tree had all 17 tracked files
  deleted locally while upstream has them. After restoring, both pass.

Final measured state of the 913: 880 pass, 25 scaffold shells, 5 with no tests,
2 failing, 1 erroring. Zero remain unverified.

# The three genuine defects

Only three survived as real defects at upstream HEAD. Each is now fixed in its
own PR, left unmerged for review.

`isic-2660` is the most consequential, and it was invisible by construction.
`medicaldevice.phase` used `:phase/0` .. `:phase/3`; Clojure's reader requires a
keyword name to start with a non-digit, so the file was unreadable and
`clojure -M:dev:test` reported ZERO tests. A medical-device blueprint's governor
suite had never executed on the JVM, and zero tests is indistinguishable from
zero failures in any summary that counts failures. Switching to the house
convention (integer phase keys, as in `advertising.phase`) made the file readable
and immediately exposed a second bug the syntax error had been hiding: an
assertion checking its own input instead of the produced fact.

`isic-3812` had `client-manifest` filtering an atom rather than its contents, and
then -- once deref'd -- matching `:client-id` at the wrong depth, so it matched
nothing.

`isic-6492` returned a fully-populated application record of nils for an id that
does not exist, because its `(:application/id m)` guard passed on a pull that
echoes the looked-up id back. In a loan store, absent data presenting as present
is a decision-input bug, and it reached both the `Store` and `KVStore` surfaces.

# isco-* market sizing

ILOSTAT (`rplumber.ilo.org`, free, no auth) yields a defensible occupation-side
figure, parallel to what the other families use -- World Bank sector value-added
for `isic-*`, country GDP for `iso3166-*`:

    annual labour income = World employment in the ISCO-08 major group
                           x median monthly USD earnings for that group
                           x 12

Employment comes from the ILO `World` aggregate (`EMP_TEMP_SEX_OCU_NB_A`, 2025),
so it is not a sum over countries and cannot double-count regional aggregates --
an earlier attempt that summed `ref_area` rows produced 24.5 billion 'workers'
against a real global figure near 3.5 billion, which is what caught the error.
Earnings come from `EAR_EMTA_SEX_OCU_CUR_NB_A` filtered to
`Currency: U.S. dollars`, a countries-only series (161 areas, no aggregates).

Two limits are recorded on every isco row rather than smoothed over: the series
resolves only to 1-digit major group, so all repos sharing a first digit tie; and
the earnings median is unweighted across reporting countries which skew rich, so
the figures are biased UPWARD. Groups 0, 6 and 9 are absent from the World series
in every year, so 32 rows stay `unscored`.

This moves 219 of 251 isco rows from 'no number' to a real cited one, and raises
scored rows from 623 to 877 of 1,226.

# Pricing the recommendation

The ranking's raw top rows are giant-economy market-entry blueprints
(`iso3166-usa`, `-chn`, `-deu`) and telecom -- the largest markets and the worst
zero-capital picks, since they need credentials, indemnity or licences. Filtered
to what one operator with no capital can sell, `isic-7310` (advertising) is the
pick: #24 of the 210 individually-researched rows, against a real ~$1.0-1.26T
global ad spend, needing no licence, inventory or float, and selling to agencies
that already bill monthly.

A survey of all 439 `isic-*` blueprints found only 10 carrying a concrete price,
and 7310 was not among them -- five revenue lines named, no numbers. The gap to
revenue was never code. A price ladder is now proposed (Managed Starter
JPY 80,000/mo, matching the tier already live on `isic-6399`, so the portfolio
shows one ladder rather than one per blueprint), anchored to public 2026
comparables: campaign-automation SaaS at $49-890/mo and managed-ads retainers at
$500-15,000+/mo.

It is deliberately NOT priced as the industry-standard 2-5% of ad spend. That
model pays the operator more precisely when the client spends more, which
contradicts the one rule the Campaign Governor enforces: a media spend over its
authorized budget forces a hold, not an override.

No Stripe object was created. That is a payment-rail action reserved for the
owner, so the blueprint has a published price and no checkout. Portfolio-wide
`externalPaid` remains 0 -- the rail is proven live (Stripe live mode, webhook
ready, a 6399 Payment Link walked end-to-end to the email step) and the final
Subscribe click has simply never been made. Per the BMC projection
(`90-docs/business/maturity-scores.edn`, as-of 2026-07-20) that leaves
`revenue = 1` and `distribution = 2` as the two binding constraints, and neither
is an engineering task.

# Consequences

- Any future portfolio sweep must execute suites and record which tree it ran
  against. A sweep of local checkouts alone is not evidence about the repos.
- `readiness` values in the ranking are now falsifiable: re-run the suite.
- isco scores differentiate 7 major groups, not 251 occupations. Anyone reading
  a per-occupation ranking off those rows is reading a tie as a signal.
- 25 scaffold shells (mostly `iso3166-*` agency subdivisions such as `jpn-*` and
  `usa-*`) are confirmed unbuilt at upstream, not merely unmarked. Their high
  raw scores come from reusing whole-country GDP for an agency, which is already
  labelled `coarse-proxy(country-level reused for agency)`.
- `orgs/kotoba-lang/identity` was found with its entire tracked working tree
  deleted locally. Restored here, but the cause is unknown and other libraries
  may be in the same state; a checkout-integrity pass over `orgs/` is worth
  running separately.")))
